# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Quarto render output. Note that each notebook's _freeze/ is deliberately NOT
# ignored: it holds the frozen results of each entry, so a fresh clone renders
# without re-running any optimizations.
aircraft-notebook/_site/
aircraft-notebook/.quarto/
optimised-glider-notebook/_site/
optimised-glider-notebook/.quarto/

# Rendered pages and their assets. These land NEXT TO the source rather than in
# _site/ when a page is rendered on its own, so the _site/ rules above miss them
# -- which is how seventeen of them once reached history via `git add -A`.
#
# The contrast with _freeze/ is the point: _freeze/ holds the frozen RESULT of
# each entry and is committed deliberately, because regenerating it means
# re-running solves that take 300-500 s. These are presentation output, rebuilt
# from _freeze/ plus source in seconds. Same tree, opposite economics.
#
# site_libs/ is anchored to a notebook root on purpose: a bare `site_libs/`
# would also match _freeze/site_libs/, which lives inside the tree that is
# deliberately kept.
*/chapters/**/*.html
*/chapters/**/*_files/
/*/site_libs/

# Draft/probe work, never committed. Each notebook has its own; the leading
# underscore is what keeps Quarto from rendering it as part of the project.
# CONTENTS, not the directory: git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so `_scratch/` plus a negation silently does nothing.
# And `**/` because a slash anywhere but the end anchors a pattern to this
# file's directory -- `_scratch/*` would match only at the repo root.
**/_scratch/*
# NO NEGATIONS HERE ANY MORE, and the reason they existed is worth keeping.
# `_scratch/_probe_base.py` was every notebook's chapter loader, and ignoring it
# globally left no notebook reproducible from git -- rule 11 required the file
# and preflight refused to start without it, so a fresh clone could not run
# anything until somebody restored it by hand. Found by checking out an old
# commit and watching it fail. The two notebooks re-included here belonged to
# the design-notebook skill, which owned its own copy and its own rule 11.
#
# That skill and both of its notebooks are gone. `nb` never used the file:
# probes run in a kernel `nb` starts and the chapter loader is
# `nb/tools/probe_init.py`, which ships with `nb`. So nothing needs re-including
# -- and nothing should get one back.

# macOS
.DS_Store
